1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,730
One of the things that we know about all types
of crime is that they are highly concentrated

2
00:00:04,730 --> 00:00:07,110
in a small number of places.

3
00:00:07,110 --> 00:00:10,340
But why is crime concentrated in so few places?

4
00:00:10,340 --> 00:00:15,040
The simplest answer to that question is that
opportunities for different types of crime

5
00:00:15,040 --> 00:00:17,470
concentrate in some places more than others.

6
00:00:17,470 --> 00:00:22,000
Shoplifting tends to concentrate on streets
with lots of shops, especially those shops

7
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:24,570
that sell items that are easy to steal.

8
00:00:24,570 --> 00:00:29,869
Meanwhile, alcohol-related violence at weekends
tends to concentrate in areas with lots of

9
00:00:29,869 --> 00:00:34,200
places selling alcohol, especially bars that
don’t mind selling drinks to people who

10
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:36,790
are already drunk.

11
00:00:36,790 --> 00:00:41,020
Sometimes a particular type of place will
create opportunities for multiple different

12
00:00:41,020 --> 00:00:42,500
types of crime.

13
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:47,500
For example, this street has lots of cars
parked in it and also has lots of houses,

14
00:00:47,500 --> 00:00:52,809
so there might be opportunities for both residential
burglaries and vehicle thefts.

15
00:00:52,809 --> 00:00:56,000
But not all crimes happen in the same places.

16
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,250
Fuel theft tends to concentrate along major
roads where petrol stations are sited, while

17
00:01:01,250 --> 00:01:06,150
theft of farm equipment is much more likely
to happen in the countryside.

18
00:01:06,150 --> 00:01:10,500
Saying that crime is concentrated because
opportunities for crime are concentrated might

19
00:01:10,500 --> 00:01:12,049
seem a bit obvious.

20
00:01:12,049 --> 00:01:18,330
But understanding how opportunities for crime
concentrate turns out to be really important

21
00:01:18,330 --> 00:01:21,610
in understanding and preventing crime more
generally.

22
00:01:21,610 --> 00:01:25,689
One way that we can better understand opportunities
for crime is to use a model like the routine

23
00:01:25,689 --> 00:01:27,270
activities approach.

24
00:01:27,270 --> 00:01:31,750
This model doesn’t tell us where a specific
crime will happen, but instead tells us where

25
00:01:31,750 --> 00:01:35,250
opportunities for crime are most likely to
occur.

26
00:01:35,250 --> 00:01:39,759
The routine activities approach says that
an opportunity for crime happens when a potential

27
00:01:39,759 --> 00:01:45,259
offender and a potential crime target come
together at a place, as long as there is no-one

28
00:01:45,259 --> 00:01:48,229
there who can stop that crime occurring.

29
00:01:48,229 --> 00:01:52,540
A potential offender is anyone who has the
knowledge and skills necessary to commit a

30
00:01:52,540 --> 00:01:57,729
particular crime, and who feels that committing
a crime is a valid thing to do in a given

31
00:01:57,729 --> 00:01:59,869
set of circumstances.

32
00:01:59,869 --> 00:02:04,450
A target for crime is whatever the offender
is trying to do something to.

33
00:02:04,450 --> 00:02:08,630
For a violent crime, the target is the victim
– the person being assaulted, for example.

34
00:02:08,630 --> 00:02:14,100
For property crimes, the target is whatever
the offender is trying to steal or damage.

35
00:02:14,100 --> 00:02:18,720
And for crimes like drug dealing, the target
is the person the dealer is trying to sell

36
00:02:18,720 --> 00:02:21,250
the drugs to.

37
00:02:21,250 --> 00:02:25,660
Sometimes offenders and targets will come
together without creating a crime opportunity,

38
00:02:25,660 --> 00:02:30,440
because there is someone present who can stop
any crime occurring.

39
00:02:30,440 --> 00:02:34,970
These people are known as controllers of crime,
although you might sometimes hear them referred

40
00:02:34,970 --> 00:02:37,950
to as guardians.

41
00:02:37,950 --> 00:02:42,330
Controllers are people who can make offenders
less likely to attack targets, make targets

42
00:02:42,330 --> 00:02:46,980
themselves less vulnerable, or make places
less suitable for offenders and targets to

43
00:02:46,980 --> 00:02:47,980
come together.

44
00:02:47,980 --> 00:02:52,960
In most cases controllers aren’t people
who are paid to prevent crime like police

45
00:02:52,960 --> 00:02:59,030
officers – usually controllers are people
who prevent crime incidentally while going

46
00:02:59,030 --> 00:03:01,750
about their day-to-day activities.

47
00:03:01,750 --> 00:03:06,390
For example, a potential burglar’s partner
might insist they go to the cinema on a particular

48
00:03:06,390 --> 00:03:11,620
evening, incidentally stopping the person
finding any suitable burglary targets.

49
00:03:11,620 --> 00:03:16,190
Likewise, if someone travels on a late-night
train looking for a sleeping passenger they

50
00:03:16,190 --> 00:03:21,180
can sexually assault, the presence of other
passengers might make them decide the risk

51
00:03:21,180 --> 00:03:24,460
of offending is too high.

52
00:03:24,460 --> 00:03:29,190
Sometimes the routine activities approach
is represented using the crime triangle.

53
00:03:29,190 --> 00:03:35,230
The basic idea here is that all three sides
of the triangle – offenders [PAUSE] targets

54
00:03:35,230 --> 00:03:41,611
[PAUSE] and places – influence how many
opportunities for crime there will be, with

55
00:03:41,611 --> 00:03:45,879
controllers able to influence each side of
the triangle.

56
00:03:45,879 --> 00:03:51,159
Since offenders and targets are both concentrated
in particular places, we can use this to explain

57
00:03:51,159 --> 00:03:54,640
why different crimes concentrate in some places
more than others.

58
00:03:54,640 --> 00:04:00,180
But it’s important to remember that different
combinations of offenders, targets and controllers

59
00:04:00,180 --> 00:04:05,010
create different opportunities for different
crimes, which explains why we see different

60
00:04:05,010 --> 00:04:07,700
types of crime cluster in different places.

61
00:04:07,700 --> 00:04:12,890
This is important because it means that we
will usually need to analyse different types

62
00:04:12,890 --> 00:04:18,290
of crime separately, for example by creating
separate maps for different crime types rather

63
00:04:18,290 --> 00:04:22,330
than showing multiple types of crime on a
single map.

64
00:04:22,330 --> 00:04:23,330
To summarise:

65
00:04:23,330 --> 00:04:29,220
Opportunities for crime are concentrated in
some places more than others

66
00:04:29,220 --> 00:04:33,910
Different crimes concentrate in different
places because different features of the environment

67
00:04:33,910 --> 00:04:37,720
create opportunities for different crimes

68
00:04:37,720 --> 00:04:42,130
We can use the routine activities approach
to help understand why crime concentrates

69
00:04:42,130 --> 00:04:43,330
in particular places